<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tbilisi State University</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ivane Javakhishvili Tbilisi State University</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> State University is the oldest university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, founded in Tbilisi in 1918 by a group of scholars led by the historian Ivane Javakhishvili, whose name it now bears. Established in the first year of the independent Democratic Republic of Georgia, it taught in Georgian and became the country's main centre of higher education and research.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tbilisi-state-university.jpg" alt="Tbilisi State University" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dimitri Shevardnadze · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TSU_Logo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tbilisi State University was established in 1918, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> had declared itself an independent national democratic state, through the leadership of the historian Ivane Javakhishvili and a group of followers. Among the co-founders were the scientists Giorgi Akhvlediani, Shalva Nutsubidze, Dimitri Uznadze, Grigol Tsereteli, Akaki Shanidze and Andrea Razmadze, and Elizabeth Orbeliani became the first woman to teach there. The chemist Petre Melikishvili was the first rector. Excluding academies and theological seminaries, which had worked in Georgia for centuries, it was the first university in Georgia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, and at the time the only institution of its kind in the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The university is housed in the former Georgian Nobility Gymnasium, built by the architect Simon Kldiashvili from 1899 to 1906. Niko Tskhvedadze, a founder of the Society for the Spreading of Literacy among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, oversaw its construction. The university opened on the day of remembrance of the Georgian king David the Builder, with a single faculty, that of philosophy, and Javakhishvili gave the first lecture. At the start of 1918 it had 18 professors and lecturers, 369 students and 89 free listeners.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> had a tradition of education, in the School of Philosophy and Rhetoric of Phazisi in Colchis in the fourth century and the Gelati and Iqalto academies of the eleventh and twelfth centuries, but as a colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> it had no national institution of higher education for centuries. Through the Bolshevik and Communist period, despite forced ideology and fierce censorship, the university kept schools in mathematics, psychology, philosophy, linguistics and historiography, and it encouraged the foundation of the Academy of Science of Georgia. In 1989 it was named after Javakhishvili.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The university has no central campus, and its buildings are spread through the city; the oldest, building number 1, houses the administration. Its yard holds the Pantheon of Tbilisi State University, where its founders are buried. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press was set up in 1923, a publishing house in 1933 and the university archive in 1933, and its scholarly Proceedings have appeared since 1919. Medical education was restored in 1994. It now has seven faculties, among them humanities, law, economics and business, and medicine, and branches in cities such as Sokhumi, Poti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zugdidi">Zugdidi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tbilisi State University” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governed by the Social Democratic (Menshevik) party under Noe Zhordania, the republic held democratic elections, carried out land reform, founded <b>Tbilisi State University</b> in 1918 and adopted a constitution in 1921.</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-republic-of-georgia">Democratic Republic of Georgia</a></li></ul></section>
